  "text": "מעניקiations\n\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step-by-step.\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the given expressions\nWe are given the following four expressions:\n\\[\nAP = {{\\cos \\alpha} \\over {\\cos 3 \\alpha}} = {{\\cos \\alpha} \\over {\\cos 11 \\alpha}} = {{\\cos \\alpha} \\over {\\cos t \\alpha}}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Simplifying the expressions\nFrom the given condition, we can deduce that:\n\\[\n\\cos \\alpha, \\cos 3 \\alpha, \\cos 11 \\alpha, \\cos t \\alpha\n\\]\nform a geometric progression (GP) where each term is the reciprocal of the previous term. This means:\n\\[\n{{\\cos \\alpha} \\over {\\cos 3 \\alpha}} = {{\\cos 3 \\alpha} \\over {\\cos 11 \\alpha}} = {{\\cos 11 \\alpha} \\over {\\cos t \\alpha}} = r\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Finding the value of $r$\nFrom the GP relationship, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos \\alpha \\cdot \\cos t \\alpha = \\cos 3 \\alpha \\cdot \\cos 11 \\alpha\n\\]\n\nUsing the trigonometric identity for the cosine of the sum and difference of angles, we can rewrite the equality:\n\\[\n\\cos (\\alpha + t \\alpha) = \\cos (\\alpha - \\alpha)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos (1 + t) \\alpha = \\cos 0\n\\]\n\nThus, we get two cases:\n\\[\n(1 + t) \\alpha = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad (1 + t) \\alpha = 2k\\pi \\quad \\text{(where \\(k\\) is an integer)}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(0 < \\alpha < \\pi\\), the second case does not apply. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n(1 + t) \\alpha = 0 \\implies t = -1\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Verifying the other expressions\nTo ensure the correctness of the solution, we verify that the expressions \\(\\cos \\alpha, \\cos 3 \\alpha, \\cos 11 \\alpha, \\cos (-\\alpha)\\) form a geometric progression:\n\\[\n\\cos \\alpha, \\cos 3 \\alpha, \\cos 11 \\alpha, \\cos (-\\alpha) = \\cos \\alpha\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos \\alpha = \\cos \\alpha\\) is trivially true, the solution is verified.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe least positive value of \\(t\\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
